    "narrative": "For Ghost Ride the WHIP today, the strategy centers on capitalizing on soft opposing rotations to boost our offensive output. **Corbin Carroll** (87.14) is our clear centerpiece; his matchup against Miles Mikolas is an absolute green light, bolstered by an elite 10% order bonus and a favorable SP delivery profile that projects to keep him active on the basepaths. Similarly, **Isaac Paredes** (72.13) and **Austin Riley** (71.27) are high-floor anchors today. **Paredes** benefits significantly from the 19% SP skill boost provided by Erick Fedde\u2019s projected struggles, while **Riley** exploits a massive 16% park factor advantage, making him a prime candidate for power production despite a modest lineup position.\n\nWe are navigating a few high-variance situations where the lineup card will be the final arbiter of value. While **Gabriel Moreno** (70.58) and **James Wood** (69.70) are currently projected as strong starts, their scores are tied to lineup assumptions that must hold to remain viable. Conversely, our toughest bench decision involved **Josh Naylor** (49.91); he was the highest-rated exclusion, but his upside was severely muted by the 19% penalty associated with facing a pitcher of Jacob deGrom's caliber. We opted for the volume of **Mickey Moniak** and **Henry Bolte** in better matchups, favoring their platoon advantages over the raw talent of **Naylor** against an elite arm.\n\nWe are keeping a close watch on the matchup between **Jose Caballero** and Cristopher S\u00e1nchez. While **Caballero** is a vital asset for our scarce stolen base category, his score reflects significant drag from a tough SP matchup and his spot in the bottom third of the order. Investors should monitor for any late-breaking lineup shifts; the thin margins between **Brice Turang** (56.65) and **Vinnie Pasquantino** (55.88) mean that even minor adjustments in batting order or confirmed weather reports could reorder our priority list as we approach first pitch.",
